00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:47.990
And she is also the chief liaison

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:50.280
for all of our affinity groups.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.790
So with that, I'm looking
forward to this conversation

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:53.623
as many of you are,

00:02:53.623 --> 00:02:55.880
and I'm gonna turn it
over to Debbie and Doug.

00:02:57.719 --> 00:03:01.770
- Welcome everyone, and a
particular welcome to you, Doug.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:05.180
Thanks for agreeing to be
in conversation with me.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:06.536
- Thank you.

00:03:06.536 --> 00:03:09.480
- I also understand that you've
agreed to be in conversation

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:10.980
with all of our participants.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.800
So I want to remind
all of our participants

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.630
that there's an opportunity for
them to contribute questions

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:19.000
during this presentation and discussion

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:20.870
using the question and answer function

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:22.870
at the bottom of the screen.

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:26.390
So Doug, during our time
together, I've got questions,

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:28.490
a host of questions that are gonna fall

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:30.520
into five different categories.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:32.860
The first is gonna be a
broad set of questions

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:35.130
that ground us in this discussion.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:37.270
The second is I'm hoping to delve

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:39.724
into the Connecticut Parentage Act.

00:03:39.724 --> 00:03:44.420
And then I thought we'd move
over to maybe understanding

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:47.280
how you were able to get the law passed

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:51.490
and then specifically about
law and your scholarship.

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:53.800
And then finally, I'd love to close us out

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:57.170
with some guidance and some
advice that you might have

00:03:57.170 --> 00:04:00.420
that we can implement long
after today's discussion.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.310
So I hope that's the
journey that you're on.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:05.600
It's the one that I'm hoping
we're gonna go on together.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:06.433
- Great.

00:04:06.433 --> 00:04:07.550
- So let me start, thank you.

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:10.140
Let me start with really a broad question

00:04:11.262 --> 00:04:15.762
before we get into the
Connecticut Parentage Act.

00:04:15.762 --> 00:04:18.200
Can you just tell us what parentage is?

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.112
It's not a term that most of use

00:04:20.112 --> 00:04:22.960
with any degree of regularity.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:25.450
- Yeah, so in some ways a term of art,

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:27.210
but parentage simply refers

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:29.350
to the legal parent-child relationship.

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:34.090
And so our laws have up until this point,

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:38.130
generally use the term
paternity and maternity,

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:39.920
but the gender neutral term, parentage

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:42.690
is what our law is going to
use now simply to describe

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:44.663
the status of being a legal parent

00:04:44.663 --> 00:04:47.140
or a child of a legal parent.

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:51.210
- Okay, fantastic and
why is this important?

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:55.370
- Yeah, so a lot comes from having

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:56.806
a legal parent-child relationship,

00:04:56.806 --> 00:05:00.900
so there's a host of parental rights

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:04.290
but also responsibilities
that come with parentage.

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:06.380
So if you are a legal parent,

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:11.380
you have decision making
authority to make decisions about

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:12.790
where your child goes to school

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:14.640
and what healthcare your child gets.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:19.560
And you also, if you and the other person

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:22.490
who might be the parent
aren't living together,

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:25.480
you have the ability to seek custody

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:27.770
or visitation with your child.

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:29.260
You also have an obligation

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:30.830
to financially support your child,

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:32.170
at least until they're 18.

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:36.160
So it's not just rights, it's
important responsibilities.

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:38.470
And then a lot of other benefits

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:41.740
turn on having a legal
parent-child relationship,

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:44.880
federal and state
benefits, social security,

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:48.840
survivor benefits, access to
things like health insurance

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:50.040
for a child.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:51.800
And so having that legal status

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:54.550
is really important to families.

00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:56.830
- Thank you and I imagine that

00:05:56.830 --> 00:05:58.480
there are many of our participants

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:00.110
that are probably taking their own notes

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:02.205
because as you and I
engage in conversation,

00:06:02.205 --> 00:06:04.960
not only will I walk
away with new knowledge,

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:06.920
I'm hoping they will as well.

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:08.910
So Doug, if we were to turn our attention

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:12.450
specifically to the
Connecticut Parentage Act,

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:15.993
can you give us a brief
summary of what it actually is?

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:19.510
- Yeah, so I guess, let me take off

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:20.770
from what we were just talking about,

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:23.700
which is for most people they
don't have to think twice

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:27.620
about the fact that they're a
legal parent of their child.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:31.530
And for those folks, this law
doesn't really change much,

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:34.870
but there are a lot of people
in Connecticut and elsewhere

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:37.500
who are raising their child

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:41.580
and yet the law might not
treat them as a legal parent.

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:44.910
And some people are aware of this

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:48.060
and they try and do
things to get around it.

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:49.330
Other people might never know

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:51.530
that they actually
aren't legally recognized

00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:54.340
because everyone else is
treating them like a parent.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:56.830
So what the Connecticut
Parentage Act tries to do

00:06:56.830 --> 00:07:01.550
is ensure that all children in
Connecticut have equal access

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:04.750
to the security of that legal
parent-child relationship

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:07.330
by having a more comprehensive

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:10.850
and inclusive parentage
framework in our law

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:13.630
so that people who
otherwise might be treated

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:15.920
as legal strangers to their children,

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:19.970
will now be treated as legal
parents to their children.

00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:22.060
So I can walk through a few of the ways

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:23.836
in which that happens.

00:07:23.836 --> 00:07:25.700
- That would be very helpful, thank you.

00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:29.910
- So for same-sex couples
ordinarily you will have

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:31.620
one of the parents

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:34.550
not have a genetic
connection to the child.

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:37.150
And so if we have a legal framework

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:41.850
that is designed around biology,
which most legal frameworks

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:44.170
around parent-child relationships are,

00:07:44.170 --> 00:07:46.700
that means for LGBTQ parents,

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:50.970
half of them are going to
not be treated as parents.

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:52.863
And so we had cases in Connecticut

00:07:52.863 --> 00:07:56.810
where you had a same-sex
couple who had a child together

00:07:56.810 --> 00:07:58.750
through donor insemination.

00:07:58.750 --> 00:08:02.790
So they use donor sperm
and raised the kid together

00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:04.250
and then they break up,

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:06.750
let's say the kid's six or eight or 10.

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:09.620
The non-biologic mother had no ability

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:11.550
to even get into court

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:14.650
and say that she was a parent
of the child and seek custody.

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:16.590
And so the law now changes that.

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:20.940
And so now it says, yes,
biology is still a basis

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:23.700
on which to establish parentage,

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:25.970
but there's other basis as well.

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:30.750
And I tend to talk about
these as intent and function.

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:33.890
So intent is take that same sex couple,

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:37.277
they are having a child
together with donor sperm.

00:08:37.277 --> 00:08:40.140
They both intend to be
parents of the child,

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:42.380
we should treat them both
as parents of the child.

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:45.770
If a heterosexual, married
couple had a child that way,

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:47.640
our law was treating them both as parents,

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:50.690
we should do the same
thing for same-sex couples

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:53.088
and we should do the same
thing for unmarried couples.

00:08:53.088 --> 00:08:56.620
The other dimension is
what I call function.

00:08:56.620 --> 00:08:59.000
And that's recognizing someone as a parent

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:01.270
because they're actually
parenting a child,

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:03.570
they've developed a
parent-child relationship.

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:06.640
So we had a wonderful family who testified

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:08.200
in support of the act

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:10.890
and they were a married same-sex couple.

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:14.110
And they had a child
together with donor sperm,

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:15.870
but one the women also had a child

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:18.840
from a previous different
sex relationship.

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:23.840
And that child was three
when the mom got together

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:26.930
with her partner and the child was now 14

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:28.940
when the bill was up and he testified

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:30.230
and supported the bill.

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:32.800
And he had been being
raised by these two moms.

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:33.980
And he called them both mom

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:36.340
and his father wasn't in the picture.

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:38.910
And under our existing law
there was no way for him

00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:42.630
to get the security of a legal
parent-child relationship

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:44.670
with his non-biologic mother

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:46.540
and intent wouldn't do him any good

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:49.862
because she wasn't there
when he was conceived.

00:09:49.862 --> 00:09:52.910
And so we have now in the law

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:55.070
something called de facto parentage,

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:57.050
which will take effect July 1.

00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:59.415
And that's essentially
treating people as parents

00:09:59.415 --> 00:10:02.560
because they've developed
a parent-child relationship

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:05.520
and are actually parenting the child.

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:09.315
So overall the act now provides routes

00:10:09.315 --> 00:10:11.390
for people to be treated as parents

00:10:11.390 --> 00:10:16.390
based on non-biologic factors
like intent and function

00:10:16.430 --> 00:10:20.360
or marriage to the birth
parent in a gender neutral way.

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:22.800
- Okay, thank you, Doug.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:25.704
So Doug, clearly I'm a bit biased

00:10:25.704 --> 00:10:27.840
and believe that this was important,

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:32.220
but overall, why was it important
to have this act passed?

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:33.850
- Yeah, so I mean, as I was saying,

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:36.720
a lot of people didn't
have a legal relationship,

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:40.170
but a lot of people were
actually remedying that

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:42.090
by adopting their own children.

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:47.060
So it was really powerful
when the chief probate judge

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:49.310
testified in support of the bill

00:10:49.310 --> 00:10:51.100
because the probate court in Connecticut

00:10:51.100 --> 00:10:53.690
has jurisdiction over adoptions.

00:10:53.690 --> 00:10:58.630
And she came in and said, "I
apologize to these families.

00:10:58.630 --> 00:11:01.100
When they come in to adopt their own kids,

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:02.700
they shouldn't have to do this."

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:08.620
And same-sex couples,
if they're being advised

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:11.290
are told do the adoption,

00:11:11.290 --> 00:11:14.220
they're still, unfortunately
are gonna be reasons

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:16.190
to think about adoption as an option,

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:18.910
given hostility in other states.

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:21.559
But I think we now have
the tools in Connecticut

00:11:21.559 --> 00:11:25.560
to give people the security of parentage

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:28.704
without having to go through
the adoption process.

00:11:28.704 --> 00:11:31.741
And it's important
because it also means that

00:11:31.741 --> 00:11:34.076
kids will have the security of having

00:11:34.076 --> 00:11:38.060
a legal parent-child
relationship so that they have

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:40.970
the right maintain relationships
with their parents.

00:11:40.970 --> 00:11:44.570
And if one parent dies,
they aren't then taken

00:11:44.570 --> 00:11:47.810
into state custody or
given to a family member,

00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:50.170
they're able to stay with their parent.

00:11:50.170 --> 00:11:54.220
There's just been too many
heart wrenching cases in which

00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:56.190
children's relationships with the people

00:11:56.190 --> 00:11:59.290
who are parenting them are
not respected by our law.

00:11:59.290 --> 00:12:03.010
And it's important that our
law actually respect families

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:04.573
as they exist in our state.

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:09.360
- Doug, you briefly
mentioned other states.

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:12.100
So I'm wondering if you
could talk with us about

00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:16.411
the process prior to
getting this act passed.

00:12:16.411 --> 00:12:19.540
In Connecticut were we leading or lagging?

00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:24.170
- Yeah, so the act is
modeled on a uniform act,

00:12:24.170 --> 00:12:26.730
the Uniform Parentage Act of 2017,

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:30.190
which I was involved with that is put out

00:12:30.190 --> 00:12:31.550
by the Uniform Law Commission,

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:34.780
which is a non-partisan
group of law professors,

00:12:34.780 --> 00:12:38.280
state lawmakers, state
court judges, and lawyers.

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:43.280
And so that was representing
the new best practices model

00:12:43.340 --> 00:12:48.140
and Washington State
adopted a version of it,

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:51.480
Maine, California, Rhode
Island, and Vermont

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:54.670
all adopted versions of it before we did.

00:12:54.670 --> 00:12:59.216
But not so bad to be really
in the forefront of that.

00:12:59.216 --> 00:13:03.190
And our mission has been
to especially try to make

00:13:03.190 --> 00:13:05.540
New England a real zone of equality here.

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:07.300
And so a version of the bill is pending

00:13:07.300 --> 00:13:09.500
in Massachusetts right now,

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:12.510
and it is being fiercely debated.

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:14.476
And we are hopeful that the
Massachusetts Legislature

00:13:14.476 --> 00:13:16.232
will pass it.

00:13:16.232 --> 00:13:19.246
But what's especially important
about Connecticut is that

00:13:19.246 --> 00:13:22.270
when you look at a state like California,

00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:24.870
California already had really good law.

00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:28.650
So it didn't take them much
to get up to speed with this.

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:32.330
Connecticut's laws had not
been updated in decades.

00:13:32.330 --> 00:13:35.870
And so our laws still
said, husband and wife,

00:13:35.870 --> 00:13:38.590
even once we allowed
same-sex couples to marry,

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:41.210
we didn't change our parentage laws.

00:13:41.210 --> 00:13:43.800
Our laws still talked about children,

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:45.190
quote, born out of wedlock.

00:13:45.190 --> 00:13:48.290
I mean, terms that we don't use anymore.

00:13:48.290 --> 00:13:50.680
And so there were various
ways in which our laws

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:52.220
just hadn't kept up.

00:13:52.220 --> 00:13:54.460
We talked about artificial insemination

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:56.280
when assisted reproduction involves

00:13:56.280 --> 00:13:58.290
a lot of different procedures now,

00:13:58.290 --> 00:14:00.520
and so Connecticut badly needed this.

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:02.100
And the most important thing was that

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:03.997
Connecticut was the only
state in New England

00:14:03.997 --> 00:14:06.940
that didn't have a path to parentage

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:11.340
for a non-biologic parent
in an unmarried couple.

00:14:11.340 --> 00:14:15.490
And so people shouldn't
have to get married

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:17.140
in a order for their
parent-child relationships

00:14:17.140 --> 00:14:18.020
to be recognized.

00:14:18.020 --> 00:14:19.920
And that was a really important advance

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:22.770
in our law given how far
we had lagged behind.

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:24.210
- Yeah, thank you, Doug.

00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:29.210
So we started with the
end, I'd like to go back

00:14:29.660 --> 00:14:31.940
and have you talk with
us about the journey.

00:14:31.940 --> 00:14:34.560
Can you talk with us about the process

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:36.830
of getting the law passed?

00:14:36.830 --> 00:14:37.960
What was the process?

00:14:37.960 --> 00:14:39.220
What was your role?

00:14:39.220 --> 00:14:41.350
What role did others play?

00:14:41.350 --> 00:14:45.910
- Yeah, so I guess I'll start by saying

00:14:45.910 --> 00:14:50.000
I had recently moved to
Yale and it was actually

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:53.593
really amazing to me the ways in which,

00:14:55.850 --> 00:14:58.710
coming from the university
allowed me to speak

00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:03.710
to a lot of folks who are
decision makers in the state

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:06.340
and talk to them about this issue.

00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:08.860
And I don't know, coming from California

00:15:08.860 --> 00:15:13.156
that I had any similar
opportunities there.

00:15:13.156 --> 00:15:15.650
So it was nice to be able to do that.

00:15:15.650 --> 00:15:18.030
So I was teaching a course called

00:15:18.030 --> 00:15:21.330
the constitution of the
family in the Law School

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:24.430
and started inviting,
I invited the new chair

00:15:24.430 --> 00:15:27.617
from the Judiciary Committee
to come speak to the class,

00:15:28.670 --> 00:15:32.230
invited the head of the
family law section of the bar

00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:34.850
and then eventually we had
the person that ended up

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:36.560
being our Chief Legislative Sponsor,

00:15:36.560 --> 00:15:38.140
Representative Jeff Currey,

00:15:38.140 --> 00:15:41.780
who carries a lot of the LGBTQ legislation

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:44.320
in the State House.

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:47.990
And the students were
able to be a part of this

00:15:47.990 --> 00:15:50.630
from the get go, which was really nice.

00:15:50.630 --> 00:15:55.350
We partnered with GLAD, which
is LGBTQ public interest firm

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:56.790
that handles New England.

00:15:56.790 --> 00:15:59.440
And so they were our main partner

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:01.873
and we created a coalition,
the Connecticut Parentage Act,

00:16:01.873 --> 00:16:06.873
we called it the We Care
Coalition and it included

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:11.785
a range of organizations and
stakeholders in the state,

00:16:11.785 --> 00:16:16.202
LGBT organizations, children's
rights organizations,

00:16:16.202 --> 00:16:20.316
healthcare providers,
assisted reproduction groups,

00:16:20.316 --> 00:16:25.316
fertility access advocacy
organizations, planned parenthood,

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:28.960
the ACLU, and really wanted to make sure

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:31.900
that we were building
this from the ground up

00:16:31.900 --> 00:16:35.290
and working with stakeholders to make sure

00:16:35.290 --> 00:16:38.170
we were able to show
lawmakers in the state

00:16:38.170 --> 00:16:39.670
why this was really important

00:16:39.670 --> 00:16:43.170
and also working with
state actors to make sure

00:16:43.170 --> 00:16:45.130
we were getting buy-in and
then working with them.

00:16:45.130 --> 00:16:49.090
So we worked very closely
with the probate court

00:16:49.090 --> 00:16:53.229
and the judicial branch, as
well as with the state agencies

00:16:53.229 --> 00:16:55.520
who deal with these matters,

00:16:55.520 --> 00:16:57.440
like the Department of Social Services

00:16:57.440 --> 00:16:59.580
and the Department of
Children and Families.

00:16:59.580 --> 00:17:03.300
And we were derailed by the
pandemic in the 2020 session,

00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:07.290
but we came back in 2021
and were able to pass it.

00:17:07.290 --> 00:17:09.920
So the nicest thing was
that some of the students

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:11.230
who had started working on it

00:17:11.230 --> 00:17:15.150
in their first year of Law
School were able to see it passed

00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:16.330
in their third year of Law School

00:17:16.330 --> 00:17:18.024
right before they graduated.

00:17:18.024 --> 00:17:21.030
- And that's an amazing
accomplishment all around.

00:17:21.030 --> 00:17:23.980
It was interesting to hear you itemize

00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:25.870
the different communities
that came together,

00:17:25.870 --> 00:17:27.800
but it's really an ecosystem.

00:17:27.800 --> 00:17:31.500
So I'm wondering, have
you had other experiences

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:36.383
prior to this trying have a law
of change or to change laws?

00:17:37.610 --> 00:17:39.950
- Yeah, so I've been very
focused on parentage.

00:17:39.950 --> 00:17:43.640
So I have been involved with
some of the other efforts

00:17:43.640 --> 00:17:45.033
in other states.

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:50.050
Connecticut was refreshing in that

00:17:50.050 --> 00:17:54.040
it was a bit more substantive
than some of the experiences

00:17:54.040 --> 00:17:55.853
we've had in other jurisdictions.

00:17:56.902 --> 00:18:01.540
People were happy to get into the weeds

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:05.340
and we included people early on.

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:09.700
I'm involved in some legislative
of work that's happening

00:18:09.700 --> 00:18:11.430
right now in Colorado.

00:18:11.430 --> 00:18:16.210
And it's a scramble because
all the stakeholders

00:18:16.210 --> 00:18:17.900
weren't there at the get go.

00:18:17.900 --> 00:18:21.300
And so I found in Connecticut
we were able to get everyone

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:26.300
to the table early on and
really be able to be thoughtful

00:18:26.750 --> 00:18:28.458
about how we were gonna do this

00:18:28.458 --> 00:18:31.240
and make sure that there weren't folks

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:33.270
coming out of the woodwork
at the last minute saying,

00:18:33.270 --> 00:18:35.430
well, what about me or I
haven't been consulted.

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:39.110
We really were exhaustive
and the legislators here

00:18:39.110 --> 00:18:41.020
I think really appreciated that.

00:18:41.020 --> 00:18:42.530
- Okay, fantastic.

00:18:42.530 --> 00:18:47.530
As you were talking you're
really referencing culture change

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:50.703
in addition to behavior
change and mindset change.

00:18:51.550 --> 00:18:55.680
We live in polarized times,
changing laws on parents

00:18:55.680 --> 00:18:58.182
and children might seem
like a culture war issue.

00:18:58.182 --> 00:19:00.879
I'm wondering if you
could talk with us about

00:19:00.879 --> 00:19:04.790
how you actually managed
to get large communities,

00:19:04.790 --> 00:19:07.480
large populations to come on board

00:19:07.480 --> 00:19:09.379
to support this particular law.

00:19:09.379 --> 00:19:14.117
And at the same, because I
heard you made a nice comparison

00:19:14.117 --> 00:19:18.630
to another example of where
people weren't early adopters.

00:19:18.630 --> 00:19:21.500
So could you talk a little
bit more about that process

00:19:21.500 --> 00:19:24.191
and roles that you had taken on,

00:19:24.191 --> 00:19:28.170
but also I imagine this is a
community building activity,

00:19:28.170 --> 00:19:30.170
what other activities occurred?

00:19:30.170 --> 00:19:35.170
- Yeah, so it was really
fascinating, I think for me,

00:19:35.190 --> 00:19:37.770
but also for my students
who at the Law School,

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:41.710
we tend to be too focused
on federal systems.

00:19:41.710 --> 00:19:43.930
We teach federal law primarily

00:19:43.930 --> 00:19:45.910
and focused on the
Supreme Court in Congress

00:19:45.910 --> 00:19:47.490
and the executive branch.

00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:49.480
And this was about the state

00:19:49.480 --> 00:19:52.810
and it was focused on
getting state lawmakers

00:19:53.890 --> 00:19:55.850
in a heavily Democratic state,

00:19:55.850 --> 00:19:58.820
but still one with a significant
Republican population

00:19:58.820 --> 00:20:01.290
in the Legislature to see
this is an important issue.

00:20:01.290 --> 00:20:04.920
And I think we were all really
heartened to see that people

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:07.080
were not just dug into their positions.

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:10.230
People were willing to
listen and be educated.

00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:15.230
And many of the law had not
thought about this issue,

00:20:15.570 --> 00:20:17.480
didn't know it was a problem,

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:20.340
but they were willing
to listen and be moved.

00:20:20.340 --> 00:20:23.280
And one example just
sticks out in my mind.

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:26.130
In our first hearing in the 2020 session,

00:20:26.130 --> 00:20:28.800
the ranking Republican
member on the Senate side

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:30.773
of the Judiciary Committee was there.

00:20:32.218 --> 00:20:33.940
We had had a meeting with him in which

00:20:33.940 --> 00:20:37.160
we told him what the bill was,
but we otherwise hadn't had

00:20:37.160 --> 00:20:39.290
any other conversations with him,

00:20:39.290 --> 00:20:44.290
but one of the families
testified and they were in tears

00:20:44.660 --> 00:20:46.560
and he was in tears.

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:49.297
And he came over to us
after the hearing and said,

00:20:49.297 --> 00:20:53.170
"I signed on as a co-sponsor
during that testimony."

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:54.950
And so nothing's more powerful, I think,

00:20:54.950 --> 00:20:56.690
than hearing from families.

00:20:56.690 --> 00:20:58.430
But you have to be willing to listen

00:20:58.430 --> 00:21:01.220
and the legislators
were willing to listen.

00:21:01.220 --> 00:21:04.970
And then the 2021 session,
there was a new House side

00:21:04.970 --> 00:21:07.920
ranking member on the Judiciary Committee.

00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:09.940
And he had founded the conservative caucus

00:21:09.940 --> 00:21:11.320
in the Legislature.

00:21:11.320 --> 00:21:13.419
And we were genuinely concerned that

00:21:13.419 --> 00:21:17.200
it might be a obstacle for us.

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:19.741
We had meetings with him, he was a lawyer,

00:21:19.741 --> 00:21:22.160
he understood the issues.

00:21:22.160 --> 00:21:26.101
And at the end, even if
he wasn't necessarily,

00:21:26.101 --> 00:21:28.613
for a lot of people the
importance of the LGBT

00:21:28.613 --> 00:21:31.450
equality dimensions of this was huge.

00:21:31.450 --> 00:21:33.910
He cared a lot about making
sure that children's lives

00:21:33.910 --> 00:21:37.080
were respected and that we were protecting

00:21:37.080 --> 00:21:39.800
their relationships and
he became a co-sponsor.

00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:43.540
And when the Governor
signed the bill on PRIDE Day

00:21:43.540 --> 00:21:47.530
in June of 2021, he was
there to witness that.

00:21:47.530 --> 00:21:51.370
And so, I mean, it's
naive of me to think that

00:21:51.370 --> 00:21:54.490
this has application in
a lot of other settings,

00:21:54.490 --> 00:21:56.310
but it was nice to see that in our state,

00:21:56.310 --> 00:21:58.300
people are still willing
to listen to each other.

00:21:58.300 --> 00:22:02.180
And we had Republican
support from the get go

00:22:02.180 --> 00:22:04.280
in both sessions.

00:22:04.280 --> 00:22:07.360
And that was actually
really important for us

00:22:07.360 --> 00:22:09.430
to be able to move the bill forward

00:22:09.430 --> 00:22:11.660
and to have people understand

00:22:11.660 --> 00:22:13.280
the importance of something like this.

00:22:13.280 --> 00:22:16.171
- Yeah and I really value storytelling.

00:22:16.171 --> 00:22:19.170
Kim in her introductory
remarks talked about

00:22:19.170 --> 00:22:20.560
the last Belonging initiative,

00:22:20.560 --> 00:22:22.920
which really focused
on telling your story.

00:22:22.920 --> 00:22:24.073
So thank you for sharing that.

00:22:24.073 --> 00:22:27.860
I'm wondering if there's
another side to this equation

00:22:27.860 --> 00:22:32.860
and that's the lens of, either
resistance or opposition.

00:22:33.460 --> 00:22:35.180
Did you experience any?

00:22:35.180 --> 00:22:37.860
And if so, how'd you work either around it

00:22:37.860 --> 00:22:39.770
or through it or conquer it?

00:22:39.770 --> 00:22:41.980
- Yeah, so the depressing
thing to me was that

00:22:41.980 --> 00:22:46.980
the most resistance were from
lawyers who don't like change.

00:22:48.440 --> 00:22:53.440
And so the community
of lawyers who practice

00:22:54.080 --> 00:22:56.920
in these cutting edge areas
of assisted reproduction

00:22:56.920 --> 00:22:59.260
and LGBT family formation in the state,

00:22:59.260 --> 00:23:01.720
it's a very small group
and they were wonderful

00:23:01.720 --> 00:23:03.400
and helpful and supportive.

00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:06.343
But the general population
of family lawyers,

00:23:06.343 --> 00:23:09.470
it took more time to get them to a place

00:23:09.470 --> 00:23:11.950
where they were comfort with this.

00:23:11.950 --> 00:23:16.940
And I think for folks who are
the ones that are doing cases

00:23:17.790 --> 00:23:22.550
and having, the bill is 177 pages long.

00:23:22.550 --> 00:23:26.420
So people quite reasonably
don't like to have

00:23:26.420 --> 00:23:30.130
to get up their seat, and even
with the courts were saying

00:23:30.130 --> 00:23:32.854
we have to do new forms and the
Department of Public Health,

00:23:32.854 --> 00:23:35.600
we have to do new kinds of forms here

00:23:35.600 --> 00:23:36.630
and birth certificates.

00:23:36.630 --> 00:23:40.357
And we have this very
important, what I take to be

00:23:40.357 --> 00:23:42.690
maybe the most important part of the bill.

00:23:42.690 --> 00:23:46.890
And that is we changed what
were previously under our law

00:23:46.890 --> 00:23:48.790
called acknowledgements of paternity

00:23:48.790 --> 00:23:50.130
to acknowledgements of parentage.

00:23:50.130 --> 00:23:53.020
So under federal law,
every state is required

00:23:53.020 --> 00:23:55.680
to have what they call
a hospital based system

00:23:55.680 --> 00:23:57.750
of paternity registration.

00:23:57.750 --> 00:24:01.130
And so that's the most
common way that paternity

00:24:01.130 --> 00:24:05.100
is established for non-marital
children in the US.

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:07.090
And so if you go to Yale, New Haven

00:24:07.090 --> 00:24:11.730
I think something like 40
to 50% of the births there

00:24:11.730 --> 00:24:15.943
to unmarried women and something
like 70% of those cases,

00:24:20.300 --> 00:24:21.560
paternity is being established

00:24:21.560 --> 00:24:23.460
through signing the
acknowledgement of paternity.

00:24:23.460 --> 00:24:25.870
The birth parent and the man who says

00:24:25.870 --> 00:24:28.410
he's the biological
father sign it together,

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:30.000
it has the force of a judgment.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:33.756
And so it establishes
paternity and has legal effect.

00:24:33.756 --> 00:24:36.070
And what we did was change it

00:24:36.070 --> 00:24:38.280
to an acknowledgement of parentage

00:24:38.280 --> 00:24:40.920
and keep the same folks can sign it,

00:24:40.920 --> 00:24:43.680
but also open it to same-sex couples

00:24:43.680 --> 00:24:45.970
and others using assisted reproduction

00:24:45.970 --> 00:24:49.500
that they could sign it to say,
they're an intended parent.

00:24:49.500 --> 00:24:52.084
And we're now the 10th state to do that.

00:24:52.084 --> 00:24:55.290
And the Department of Public Health

00:24:55.290 --> 00:24:57.310
wasn't thrilled about this,
'cause they have forms

00:24:57.310 --> 00:24:58.350
and computer systems.

00:24:58.350 --> 00:25:02.010
And so really, it took
getting people to see

00:25:02.010 --> 00:25:04.170
we're gonna do this and
so come along with us

00:25:04.170 --> 00:25:06.783
so that we can make it
as painless as possible,

00:25:07.670 --> 00:25:08.940
but it's gonna happen.

00:25:08.940 --> 00:25:11.100
And it's important that it happens

00:25:11.100 --> 00:25:15.910
and concerns about changing
forms or learning new law

00:25:15.910 --> 00:25:19.803
are not sufficient reasons to not do this.

00:25:19.803 --> 00:25:23.160
So that was where we had to
deal with the most resistance.

00:25:23.160 --> 00:25:27.183
- Yeah, so let me move from
resistance to the word fear.

00:25:28.390 --> 00:25:31.670
Were there any conversations
that you were a part of

00:25:31.670 --> 00:25:35.940
or learned about where part of
the resistance may have been

00:25:35.940 --> 00:25:39.140
someone's personal fear or in other cases,

00:25:39.140 --> 00:25:42.380
maybe lack of knowledge about
this particular community

00:25:42.380 --> 00:25:45.940
and the goals of this project?

00:25:45.940 --> 00:25:48.320
- Yeah, so what's what was
interesting in Connecticut,

00:25:48.320 --> 00:25:52.090
so I think in other states
the fact that it protects

00:25:52.090 --> 00:25:55.540
LGBTQ families would be a problem

00:25:55.540 --> 00:26:00.120
and there might be reasons not
to promote parentage reform

00:26:00.120 --> 00:26:01.650
in that way.

00:26:01.650 --> 00:26:05.640
In Connecticut, our
legislators and lots of people

00:26:05.640 --> 00:26:07.610
in the state were actually eager,

00:26:07.610 --> 00:26:09.870
they got it when we talk
talked about those families.

00:26:09.870 --> 00:26:13.680
Yeah, my friend or my
neighbor had to do that

00:26:13.680 --> 00:26:16.150
and they shouldn't have to do that,

00:26:16.150 --> 00:26:18.390
but once you start digging into it,

00:26:18.390 --> 00:26:20.040
so for instance, we have a provision in

00:26:20.040 --> 00:26:24.500
there that allows a court to recognize

00:26:24.500 --> 00:26:26.213
more than two parents for a child.

00:26:27.110 --> 00:26:31.090
And that made some people
uneasy and I think it is fear.

00:26:31.090 --> 00:26:32.127
And people started to say,

00:26:32.127 --> 00:26:36.290
"Well, what if a heterosexual
identified man says,

00:26:36.290 --> 00:26:38.510
'What if my wife divorces me

00:26:38.510 --> 00:26:39.510
and gets married to another man

00:26:39.510 --> 00:26:41.240
and he says he's the parent?'"

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:45.900
And you have to educate folks
on who these families are.

00:26:45.900 --> 00:26:48.943
In my own research I study
some multi parent families

00:26:48.943 --> 00:26:53.850
and courts actually are only doing this

00:26:53.850 --> 00:26:57.616
when the person really is
the parent of the child

00:26:57.616 --> 00:27:02.616
and it's not being used by
stepparents to assert parentage.

00:27:02.850 --> 00:27:07.020
And so I think people get
worried when they think that

00:27:07.020 --> 00:27:09.350
things are changing, but what
they sometimes fail to see

00:27:09.350 --> 00:27:11.110
is things have already changed.

00:27:11.110 --> 00:27:12.840
We just haven't really caught up to it.

00:27:12.840 --> 00:27:15.500
And so these families all exist.

00:27:15.500 --> 00:27:16.850
People might not think of them.

00:27:16.850 --> 00:27:19.740
And sometimes they're the
most vulnerable families.

00:27:19.740 --> 00:27:22.420
The reason there's a
third parent is often that

00:27:22.420 --> 00:27:26.440
the biological parents
were facing challenges

00:27:26.440 --> 00:27:28.050
that made it difficult to parent.

00:27:28.050 --> 00:27:30.480
And so it's about protecting the children

00:27:30.480 --> 00:27:31.610
in these families.

00:27:31.610 --> 00:27:34.140
And I think having
people come back to that

00:27:34.140 --> 00:27:35.390
and see that these families exist,

00:27:35.390 --> 00:27:38.080
that's why the family
stories are so important,

00:27:38.080 --> 00:27:39.610
helps deal with some of the fear,

00:27:39.610 --> 00:27:42.030
which is just fear of the unknown

00:27:42.030 --> 00:27:44.070
and fear of unintended consequences.

00:27:44.070 --> 00:27:45.557
- Yeah, thank you, thank you.

00:27:45.557 --> 00:27:47.803
I love the power of the storytelling.

00:27:48.810 --> 00:27:52.130
You talked about different partners.

00:27:52.130 --> 00:27:54.630
I'm wondering if we
could focus a little bit

00:27:54.630 --> 00:27:57.580
on your law students.

00:27:57.580 --> 00:28:00.390
You had partners in this
work, you led a clinic,

00:28:00.390 --> 00:28:02.400
you referenced your law students.

00:28:02.400 --> 00:28:05.260
Could you share with me and us

00:28:05.260 --> 00:28:08.300
what the role of the
clinic was and specifically

00:28:08.300 --> 00:28:11.390
the role that your law students played?

00:28:11.390 --> 00:28:15.212
- Yeah, so I should start by
saying I'm not a clinician,

00:28:15.212 --> 00:28:18.860
but Dean Gerken set an
example at the Law School

00:28:18.860 --> 00:28:21.800
of those of us on the doctrinal side

00:28:21.800 --> 00:28:24.130
being able to do clinics
when it intersects

00:28:24.130 --> 00:28:25.130
with work we're doing.

00:28:25.130 --> 00:28:29.690
And I was a practicing lawyer
before becoming an academic

00:28:29.690 --> 00:28:32.720
and got licensed in
Connecticut to make sure

00:28:32.720 --> 00:28:34.262
I could do this work.

00:28:34.262 --> 00:28:38.870
And so I started a legislative
advocacy clinic on this

00:28:38.870 --> 00:28:43.610
and had students working on
it across the three years

00:28:43.610 --> 00:28:45.910
we were doing it, including
the two sessions in which

00:28:45.910 --> 00:28:47.280
it was introduced.

00:28:47.280 --> 00:28:48.830
Some of those students carried over.

00:28:48.830 --> 00:28:50.810
So we had institutional memory

00:28:50.810 --> 00:28:53.700
and they were able to lead
teams in the last year.

00:28:53.700 --> 00:28:56.852
The really wonderful thing
is that at the Law School,

00:28:56.852 --> 00:28:59.780
I had students who, before
coming to Law School,

00:28:59.780 --> 00:29:01.710
their job was in public interest,

00:29:01.710 --> 00:29:04.360
marketing and consulting
or communications.

00:29:04.360 --> 00:29:09.360
And so we were able to really
have students with skill sets.

00:29:10.810 --> 00:29:12.870
I'm not on social media at all.

00:29:12.870 --> 00:29:15.130
So students who could run
the social media accounts,

00:29:15.130 --> 00:29:17.300
students who could figure out how to do

00:29:18.144 --> 00:29:21.560
certain kinds of communication
strategies and press releases

00:29:21.560 --> 00:29:24.720
and get op-eds placed and
so that was wonderful.

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:28.220
And then we had students doing
more traditional legal work.

00:29:28.220 --> 00:29:29.530
We needed to research things.

00:29:29.530 --> 00:29:32.000
We needed to go through
the Connecticut statutes

00:29:32.000 --> 00:29:35.270
and amend all of the
statutes that are affected

00:29:35.270 --> 00:29:37.420
because there's a bunch of statutes

00:29:37.420 --> 00:29:40.110
that use terms like paternity and limit.

00:29:40.110 --> 00:29:43.337
So think of probate statutes,
we wanna make sure that

00:29:43.337 --> 00:29:46.180
if someone dies that all of the children

00:29:46.180 --> 00:29:48.130
we're having recognized
are gonna be treated

00:29:48.130 --> 00:29:50.580
as legitimate children of the decedent.

00:29:50.580 --> 00:29:52.650
And so we had to really do all of that

00:29:52.650 --> 00:29:55.113
and students were tremendously helpful

00:29:55.113 --> 00:29:57.670
and they also did a lot
of legislative advocacy,

00:29:57.670 --> 00:30:01.340
so they helped with testimony,

00:30:01.340 --> 00:30:06.340
they met with constituents and
really drove letter writing.

00:30:07.960 --> 00:30:11.820
And so they were able to
see every dimension of it

00:30:11.820 --> 00:30:15.450
and I hope really be able to do the work

00:30:15.450 --> 00:30:18.833
that they were most passionate
about and get an experience

00:30:18.833 --> 00:30:21.220
that they otherwise weren't gonna get.

00:30:21.220 --> 00:30:23.120
Since most of the other
work they were exposed to

00:30:23.120 --> 00:30:24.970
was really litigation type work

00:30:24.970 --> 00:30:29.110
and this was something different,
but they were wonderful

00:30:29.110 --> 00:30:33.320
and it made it so meaningful
to be able to get across

00:30:33.320 --> 00:30:34.610
the finish line with them.

00:30:34.610 --> 00:30:36.840
- And it sounds like an
amazing learning experience

00:30:36.840 --> 00:30:39.130
that you don't often get
a chance to replicate

00:30:39.130 --> 00:30:42.240
from year to year, so
it sounds very exciting.

00:30:42.240 --> 00:30:43.600
So Doug, I started by saying

00:30:43.600 --> 00:30:45.413
I've got five categories of questions.

00:30:45.413 --> 00:30:48.750
I'm gonna switch us to my fourth category.

00:30:48.750 --> 00:30:52.820
Which is about the law
and your scholarship.

00:30:52.820 --> 00:30:54.640
You don't need to be a lawyer to know

00:30:54.640 --> 00:30:57.880
that there's been a lot
of change in the law,

00:30:57.880 --> 00:31:01.440
especially in the last 10 years
with regard to protections

00:31:01.440 --> 00:31:02.860
for our lesbian, gay, bisexual,

00:31:02.860 --> 00:31:04.313
transgender, queer community.

00:31:05.620 --> 00:31:07.970
There seems to be more
and more protections.

00:31:07.970 --> 00:31:11.690
But I know in Florida, which
I recently visited this year

00:31:11.690 --> 00:31:15.460
and elsewhere there's current
attention to LGBTQ issues,

00:31:15.460 --> 00:31:19.000
specifically in schools and in classrooms.

00:31:19.000 --> 00:31:22.710
Can you say a bit about
the LGBTQ community

00:31:22.710 --> 00:31:26.500
and the law and its impact
and some of the decisions

00:31:26.500 --> 00:31:29.390
that some of our politicians are making?

00:31:29.390 --> 00:31:31.920
Would love to hear your
perspective on that.

00:31:31.920 --> 00:31:33.740
- Yeah, so unfortunately,

00:31:33.740 --> 00:31:36.410
I mean, as you probably would guess,

00:31:36.410 --> 00:31:39.591
parentage law in Florida's
also really dreadful

00:31:39.591 --> 00:31:44.010
for LGBTQ people and parentage on Texas

00:31:44.010 --> 00:31:46.200
is not good for LGBTQ people.

00:31:46.200 --> 00:31:49.410
So there are zones like New England

00:31:49.410 --> 00:31:54.280
where there's really equality
across multiple domains,

00:31:54.280 --> 00:31:57.708
employment, parent-child
relationships, schools,

00:31:57.708 --> 00:32:01.460
and then you get to other very
popular states like Florida

00:32:01.460 --> 00:32:05.630
and Texas in which right
now in Florida or Texas,

00:32:05.630 --> 00:32:09.280
if an unmarried lesbian
couple has a child together

00:32:09.280 --> 00:32:14.280
and breaks up, only the biological
mother is a legal parent.

00:32:14.327 --> 00:32:19.327
And in Texas, the courts were
even saying until recently

00:32:21.130 --> 00:32:24.070
that a married lesbian couple
who had a child together

00:32:24.070 --> 00:32:26.290
couldn't both be parents of that child,

00:32:26.290 --> 00:32:29.150
even though we don't require
that men married to women

00:32:29.150 --> 00:32:30.493
be a biological father.

00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:35.080
So these are states where
I think LGBTQ people

00:32:35.080 --> 00:32:39.370
and their families are
already feeling stigmatized

00:32:39.370 --> 00:32:41.260
and facing discrimination.

00:32:41.260 --> 00:32:44.973
The school's bills like
in Florida for instance,

00:32:45.920 --> 00:32:48.293
are bad for children and bad for parents.

00:32:49.760 --> 00:32:52.980
There are whether the law
recognizes them or not,

00:32:52.980 --> 00:32:57.238
there are families that include
LGBTQ parents in Florida

00:32:57.238 --> 00:32:59.380
and their kids are in school.

00:32:59.380 --> 00:33:02.650
And we have the luxury in
Connecticut of being able

00:33:02.650 --> 00:33:05.160
to talk to our schools about making sure

00:33:05.160 --> 00:33:07.490
that their curriculum is
inclusive and reflects

00:33:07.490 --> 00:33:09.700
the lives of the children
in the classroom.

00:33:09.700 --> 00:33:12.950
And now in Florida, people
don't have that luxury

00:33:12.950 --> 00:33:15.090
and those children are gonna get messages

00:33:15.090 --> 00:33:17.910
that their families don't measure up.

00:33:17.910 --> 00:33:19.830
And that's what we've been
fighting again for so long.

00:33:19.830 --> 00:33:22.450
I mean, in Texas and
it's even more horrifying

00:33:22.450 --> 00:33:26.720
given the Child Protective
Services order that parents

00:33:26.720 --> 00:33:28.092
who are allowing their children

00:33:28.092 --> 00:33:33.092
to have gender affirming
care are essentially deemed

00:33:33.500 --> 00:33:36.080
to be committing child abuse or neglect.

00:33:36.080 --> 00:33:41.080
And I mean these, in some
ways, it goes so much

00:33:41.210 --> 00:33:45.180
against conservative ideas
about parental rights

00:33:45.180 --> 00:33:48.020
and parental autonomy, it's
parental rights and autonomy

00:33:48.020 --> 00:33:50.410
for some people and not for others.

00:33:50.410 --> 00:33:55.040
And it's obviously not good for
children to be in situations

00:33:55.040 --> 00:33:59.210
where not only is their emergent
identity being stigmatized,

00:33:59.210 --> 00:34:01.690
but their are being told they can't,

00:34:01.690 --> 00:34:03.500
the thing I said is so
important to parentage,

00:34:03.500 --> 00:34:06.200
making decisions for your
child about healthcare,

00:34:06.200 --> 00:34:08.500
they can't make those decisions
and protect their children.

00:34:08.500 --> 00:34:12.340
So I mean, I'm in despair
about some of this stuff

00:34:12.340 --> 00:34:15.240
but I also know we have
people who are working on it,

00:34:15.240 --> 00:34:18.530
including folks at Yale and
at the Yale Child Study Center

00:34:18.530 --> 00:34:21.170
who are trying to get people to understand

00:34:21.170 --> 00:34:24.750
the stakes and see that
this is bad for families

00:34:24.750 --> 00:34:26.290
and bad for children.

00:34:26.290 --> 00:34:29.450
- Yeah, Doug, you use the word luxury

00:34:29.450 --> 00:34:32.240
when you were responding
to the last question.

00:34:32.240 --> 00:34:35.629
I have a note here where
early on you referenced

00:34:35.629 --> 00:34:39.760
making New England a zone of equality.

00:34:39.760 --> 00:34:41.600
I'm a native of New Haven,

00:34:41.600 --> 00:34:45.660
which makes me a native of
Connecticut and still a resident.

00:34:45.660 --> 00:34:50.270
And I have to say listening
to your talk and the outcome

00:34:50.270 --> 00:34:54.810
of the collective effort
makes me proud to not just say

00:34:54.810 --> 00:34:59.010
I'm a resident of New Haven
but certainly of Connecticut.

00:34:59.010 --> 00:35:03.770
And so I'm thinking about
work through the lens

00:35:03.770 --> 00:35:07.060
of both scholarship, but
also through a legal lens.

00:35:07.060 --> 00:35:11.630
And then also the teaching and
exposure that you've created

00:35:11.630 --> 00:35:13.220
for your students.

00:35:13.220 --> 00:35:17.910
So scholarship can sometimes
be highly theoretical

00:35:17.910 --> 00:35:21.540
dealing with the ideas rather
than the practical matters.

00:35:21.540 --> 00:35:25.240
That might be the different at
a law school, maybe, I guess,

00:35:25.240 --> 00:35:28.060
how do you see the relationship
between scholarship

00:35:29.110 --> 00:35:30.420
and the changes in law?

00:35:30.420 --> 00:35:31.360
Is there one?

00:35:31.360 --> 00:35:35.260
And if so, what's your view?

00:35:35.260 --> 00:35:39.140
- Yeah, so some of my
colleagues at the Law School

00:35:39.140 --> 00:35:41.860
would certainly say I'm not
sufficiently theoretical.

00:35:41.860 --> 00:35:43.700
So I will just start with that.

00:35:43.700 --> 00:35:46.880
I care deeply about having
effects in the world.

00:35:46.880 --> 00:35:48.480
And that's why I think a law school

00:35:48.480 --> 00:35:51.050
is actually a really good
setting for some of the things

00:35:51.050 --> 00:35:52.550
that I was most interested in.

00:35:54.837 --> 00:35:59.837
I mean, my research church is
motivated by my commitments

00:36:00.380 --> 00:36:03.110
to certain values and certain communities.

00:36:03.110 --> 00:36:08.110
And so I found myself really focusing

00:36:08.280 --> 00:36:12.550
on parent-child relationships
when I understood the harms.

00:36:12.550 --> 00:36:15.700
And so for me, it's not
abstract or theoretical.

00:36:15.700 --> 00:36:20.610
And so I was interested in
being able to show people

00:36:20.610 --> 00:36:25.610
how the parentage framework
works to marginalize some people

00:36:26.730 --> 00:36:28.710
and benefit others.

00:36:28.710 --> 00:36:32.240
I think one could have
a fairly simplistic view

00:36:32.240 --> 00:36:35.240
that just says, well,
non-biological parents,

00:36:35.240 --> 00:36:37.060
because biology's what matters.

00:36:37.060 --> 00:36:39.040
If you're not a biological parent,

00:36:39.040 --> 00:36:41.290
you're all treated the
same, you have to adopt.

00:36:41.290 --> 00:36:44.380
But what I tried to do in
my work is really dig down

00:36:44.380 --> 00:36:46.830
into the frameworks of each state

00:36:46.830 --> 00:36:48.860
'cause this is a state by state question

00:36:48.860 --> 00:36:51.220
and see that that's not really true.

00:36:51.220 --> 00:36:53.100
If you are a man married to a woman,

00:36:53.100 --> 00:36:54.950
biology isn't everything.

00:36:54.950 --> 00:36:59.100
And so it's some people who are being told

00:36:59.100 --> 00:37:01.000
they have to adopt their own children.

00:37:02.348 --> 00:37:07.348
And also biology has been
deemed more essential to women,

00:37:08.100 --> 00:37:11.530
to motherhood than to men or fatherhood.

00:37:11.530 --> 00:37:14.220
And so it carries forward ideas we've had

00:37:14.220 --> 00:37:17.310
that it's women's natural
destiny to be mothers

00:37:17.310 --> 00:37:22.010
and fathers can be
replaced by someone else.

00:37:22.010 --> 00:37:24.460
And you see that even in the
way the law has developed.

00:37:24.460 --> 00:37:29.010
And so it was much easier in
some states to get recognition

00:37:29.010 --> 00:37:32.276
for non-biological mothers
in same-sex couples

00:37:32.276 --> 00:37:35.370
because courts essentially
treated them like fathers.

00:37:35.370 --> 00:37:37.573
They satisfied paternity presumptions,

00:37:38.490 --> 00:37:42.720
but non-biologic fathers
in male same-sex couples

00:37:42.720 --> 00:37:44.090
have had a much harder time.

00:37:44.090 --> 00:37:47.740
And actually their hard time
has been like the experience

00:37:47.740 --> 00:37:51.200
of non-biologic mothers
in different sex couples.

00:37:51.200 --> 00:37:55.020
Because our law has had a
hard time saying that a woman

00:37:55.020 --> 00:37:57.723
who doesn't give birth is a parent.

00:37:58.649 --> 00:38:02.230
And so I've been motivated by
trying to get people to see

00:38:02.230 --> 00:38:05.123
that the burdens of the
system don't fall evenly.

00:38:06.270 --> 00:38:07.590
The other thing is that this work,

00:38:07.590 --> 00:38:11.850
it is a feedback loop for
me, so doing this work,

00:38:11.850 --> 00:38:14.330
the place that we encountered
the most resistance

00:38:14.330 --> 00:38:17.270
was on what I called
functional parenthood.

00:38:17.270 --> 00:38:20.730
And that is someone's a parent
because they have formed

00:38:20.730 --> 00:38:22.940
a parent-child relationship,
we have requirements,

00:38:22.940 --> 00:38:24.130
they have to reside with the child,

00:38:24.130 --> 00:38:26.020
hold the child out as their child,

00:38:26.020 --> 00:38:27.630
but they formed a
parent-child relationship.

00:38:27.630 --> 00:38:28.840
They might have come into the child's life

00:38:28.840 --> 00:38:30.150
when the child was one,

00:38:30.150 --> 00:38:31.660
they weren't there when
the child was born,

00:38:31.660 --> 00:38:33.510
but this is the child's parent.

00:38:33.510 --> 00:38:36.320
And a lot of people resisted
that because they thought,

00:38:36.320 --> 00:38:38.220
well, then anyone's gonna be a parent.

00:38:39.109 --> 00:38:41.810
And biological parents
are gonna have to have

00:38:41.810 --> 00:38:44.650
their rights infringed by these folks

00:38:44.650 --> 00:38:47.423
who are really just mere
helpers who are coming in.

00:38:48.540 --> 00:38:53.540
And it just didn't reflect
what I knew to be the families

00:38:54.160 --> 00:38:55.620
who are affected by this.

00:38:55.620 --> 00:38:59.130
And so a colleague of mine,
Courtney Jocelyn at UC Davis,

00:38:59.130 --> 00:39:00.780
who does this work as well.

00:39:00.780 --> 00:39:03.443
We decided to collect all of
the functional parent cases

00:39:03.443 --> 00:39:06.690
in the 29 jurisdictions
that have these doctrines

00:39:06.690 --> 00:39:10.640
and with a fabulous team of
research assistants at Yale,

00:39:10.640 --> 00:39:14.103
code them and be able to
see what are the cases.

00:39:15.460 --> 00:39:17.870
And then now we're taking that research,

00:39:17.870 --> 00:39:19.830
so in Massachusetts the big resistance

00:39:19.830 --> 00:39:21.820
is to the functional parent provision.

00:39:21.820 --> 00:39:26.020
And the advocates at GLAD
are sending the draft article

00:39:26.020 --> 00:39:27.780
and I'm in meetings telling them,

00:39:27.780 --> 00:39:32.780
so for instance, in 83% of
these 600 plus cases we have

00:39:32.800 --> 00:39:36.030
the person who's saying they
aren't functional parent

00:39:36.030 --> 00:39:38.370
is the child's primary caregiver, right?

00:39:38.370 --> 00:39:40.768
It's like a lot of families
facing a lot of challenges.

00:39:40.768 --> 00:39:43.434
In 20% of the cases, no legal parent

00:39:43.434 --> 00:39:48.434
has been a consistent
presence in the child's life.

00:39:48.910 --> 00:39:51.040
So it's not what people envision.

00:39:51.040 --> 00:39:53.790
It's not the step parent who's saying,

00:39:53.790 --> 00:39:57.590
I wanna be a parent too,
it's actually families,

00:39:57.590 --> 00:39:59.190
you get more of these cases

00:39:59.190 --> 00:40:01.920
in places like Kentucky and West Virginia,

00:40:01.920 --> 00:40:05.112
that sociologists are showing us

00:40:05.112 --> 00:40:07.280
because of the opioid epidemic

00:40:07.280 --> 00:40:11.170
a lot of children are not
residing with a legal parent.

00:40:11.170 --> 00:40:13.170
And so someone else is parenting them.

00:40:13.170 --> 00:40:15.360
And I guess the bigger push,

00:40:15.360 --> 00:40:17.400
I mean the sense where I
do get more theoretical,

00:40:17.400 --> 00:40:20.020
I want people to see that as parenting,

00:40:20.020 --> 00:40:22.495
like that is not just caregiving,

00:40:22.495 --> 00:40:26.317
parenting is a practice it's not a status.

00:40:26.317 --> 00:40:27.687
- And it's an investment as well.

00:40:27.687 --> 00:40:29.603
- Yes and it's hard work.

00:40:30.690 --> 00:40:35.430
- Yes, I have two children
and I agree with that.

00:40:35.430 --> 00:40:40.090
Doug, so one might look at
this accomplishment and say,

00:40:40.090 --> 00:40:43.120
you're all done, I'm not sure you are.

00:40:43.120 --> 00:40:46.270
So my next question is really about,

00:40:46.270 --> 00:40:47.640
maybe it was about yesterday,

00:40:47.640 --> 00:40:48.820
but I think it's written

00:40:48.820 --> 00:40:51.143
as if we're looking into the future.

00:40:52.130 --> 00:40:53.630
What's next?

00:40:53.630 --> 00:40:56.651
And more importantly, what's next for you?

00:40:56.651 --> 00:40:58.400
What else are you working on?

00:40:58.400 --> 00:41:02.540
- Yeah, so I think, I mean, in some ways

00:41:02.540 --> 00:41:04.640
the most basic thing
is getting more states

00:41:04.640 --> 00:41:08.380
to treat people equally and
also getting other states

00:41:08.380 --> 00:41:12.500
to respect what we're doing
in places like Connecticut.

00:41:12.500 --> 00:41:14.524
I just had a call yesterday
with lawyers who say,

00:41:14.524 --> 00:41:18.480
I can get a Connecticut judgment
and then I go to Alabama

00:41:18.480 --> 00:41:21.230
and the Alabama Department
of Vital Records says,

00:41:21.230 --> 00:41:23.120
I don't have to treat this or Tennessee,

00:41:23.120 --> 00:41:24.637
the place we were talking
about was Tennessee,

00:41:24.637 --> 00:41:26.320
I don't have to treat
this as a valid judgment,

00:41:26.320 --> 00:41:28.100
I'm not giving you a birth certificate.

00:41:28.100 --> 00:41:30.711
And so we need to really
focus on those questions

00:41:30.711 --> 00:41:34.740
of people being able to
go across state lines

00:41:36.054 --> 00:41:38.660
to hostile territory and
be treated as parents.

00:41:38.660 --> 00:41:39.780
That's a big concern for people.

00:41:39.780 --> 00:41:41.030
It's why people still adopt

00:41:41.030 --> 00:41:43.073
even when they live in friendly states,

00:41:44.240 --> 00:41:47.050
even though the adoption
decree should not be treated

00:41:47.050 --> 00:41:48.920
differently than a judgment

00:41:48.920 --> 00:41:52.593
or anything else that has a court order.

00:41:53.570 --> 00:41:56.890
So that's one thing,
the things that follow

00:41:58.160 --> 00:42:00.040
from passing parentage reform.

00:42:00.040 --> 00:42:02.000
So for instance, there have
been folks in Connecticut

00:42:02.000 --> 00:42:06.100
interested in changing
definitions of infertility

00:42:06.100 --> 00:42:09.736
so that same sex couples and
single women would have access

00:42:09.736 --> 00:42:14.120
to covered infertility treatment.

00:42:14.120 --> 00:42:16.933
And I think you'll see
that continue to take off.

00:42:20.223 --> 00:42:23.970
So assisted reproduction
has become a big focus.

00:42:23.970 --> 00:42:26.520
And so there's also
something we did in our law,

00:42:26.520 --> 00:42:29.600
but is just a start is

00:42:29.600 --> 00:42:32.130
how are we gonna regulate that industry?

00:42:32.130 --> 00:42:35.500
And how are we going
to provide information

00:42:35.500 --> 00:42:38.445
to people who have donor conceived?

00:42:38.445 --> 00:42:41.450
So making sure they get
their medical history,

00:42:41.450 --> 00:42:43.030
that they have full information,

00:42:43.030 --> 00:42:45.760
so I think that's really a next frontier.

00:42:45.760 --> 00:42:49.160
And I think that's a tricky
one because I'm committed

00:42:49.160 --> 00:42:51.360
to things like people should have access

00:42:51.360 --> 00:42:53.440
to medical information,

00:42:53.440 --> 00:42:56.010
and I'm also even committed to things like

00:42:58.660 --> 00:43:00.530
people should be counseled
about the importance

00:43:00.530 --> 00:43:03.330
of telling their children that
they were donor conceived,

00:43:03.330 --> 00:43:05.570
about thinking about
whether they want a donor

00:43:05.570 --> 00:43:07.550
who agrees to have their identity released

00:43:07.550 --> 00:43:09.166
when the child is 18.

00:43:09.166 --> 00:43:11.349
But I also think some of those discussions

00:43:11.349 --> 00:43:15.150
tend to geneticize family relationships

00:43:15.150 --> 00:43:16.660
in ways that are problematic

00:43:16.660 --> 00:43:19.741
for my own work in LGBTQ families.

00:43:19.741 --> 00:43:22.890
And so I think we need to be
clear that when we talk about

00:43:22.890 --> 00:43:25.733
relationships created
through donor gamuts,

00:43:27.420 --> 00:43:30.090
those are relationships that
could have different meanings,

00:43:30.090 --> 00:43:32.480
but we shouldn't talk
about them as father,

00:43:32.480 --> 00:43:35.040
biological fathers, parents.

00:43:35.040 --> 00:43:37.630
I think its important and we
shouldn't be thinking that

00:43:37.630 --> 00:43:40.200
children are harmed by not having

00:43:41.120 --> 00:43:42.980
both biological parents raising them.

00:43:42.980 --> 00:43:47.980
And so for me, I think an
important next bigger project

00:43:48.600 --> 00:43:50.298
is just to get people to see that

00:43:50.298 --> 00:43:53.770
biology doesn't make
parent-child relationships.

00:43:53.770 --> 00:43:56.160
And in our current world of 23 and Me

00:43:56.160 --> 00:44:00.650
where we're really fetishizing
ancestry and genetics,

00:44:00.650 --> 00:44:04.724
it's important to be able
to get people to appreciate

00:44:04.724 --> 00:44:07.350
that that's not all that family is.

00:44:07.350 --> 00:44:11.010
- Yeah, thank you, so for our audience,

00:44:11.010 --> 00:44:13.744
please feel free to type questions in.

00:44:13.744 --> 00:44:15.910
I have just a couple more questions.

00:44:15.910 --> 00:44:20.183
So trying to bring us a
little bit closer to closure.

00:44:21.030 --> 00:44:25.060
This is my fifth and final
category and I've entitled it

00:44:25.060 --> 00:44:29.710
advice and guidance,
it's kind of a two part.

00:44:29.710 --> 00:44:33.830
So what should we all know

00:44:33.830 --> 00:44:36.150
about parents and children in Connecticut?

00:44:36.150 --> 00:44:39.280
First part and then the second is,

00:44:39.280 --> 00:44:41.260
what should we tell our friends?

00:44:41.260 --> 00:44:44.650
So I'm thinking specifically
as you started to describe

00:44:44.650 --> 00:44:48.290
that ecosystem, what role can I play?

00:44:48.290 --> 00:44:51.850
What role can other members
that are either going to watch

00:44:51.850 --> 00:44:55.730
this recorded discussion
or are with us today?

00:44:55.730 --> 00:44:59.063
What actions can we take
away from today's discussion?

00:44:59.930 --> 00:45:03.560
- Yeah, so, I mean, I think
I hope people appreciate that

00:45:03.560 --> 00:45:05.600
what a lot of folks take for granted,

00:45:05.600 --> 00:45:07.590
other people spend a lot of time

00:45:07.590 --> 00:45:12.140
and energy and money making clear.

00:45:12.140 --> 00:45:17.140
And so I know people still think well, if,

00:45:17.550 --> 00:45:20.180
and the one legislator
who voted against us,

00:45:20.180 --> 00:45:22.120
he said on the House floor,

00:45:22.120 --> 00:45:25.120
'Well, can't gay people just adopt?"

00:45:25.120 --> 00:45:26.730
And that was the remedy for him.

00:45:26.730 --> 00:45:29.990
And I will say like in European countries

00:45:29.990 --> 00:45:33.320
increasingly I always say
they talk about adoption

00:45:33.320 --> 00:45:36.330
as the remedy and here we
talk about it as the harm,

00:45:36.330 --> 00:45:39.440
because you shouldn't have
to, I'm an adoptive parent,

00:45:39.440 --> 00:45:41.990
but I didn't have to adopt my own child,

00:45:41.990 --> 00:45:44.790
I adopted in the process
in which someone else

00:45:44.790 --> 00:45:46.820
is the parent at birth,

00:45:46.820 --> 00:45:50.110
but the folks that we're helping here

00:45:50.110 --> 00:45:51.150
they're adopting their own kids,

00:45:51.150 --> 00:45:53.330
no one else is saying they're
a parent to that child.

00:45:53.330 --> 00:45:56.320
And so I think people need to experience

00:45:56.320 --> 00:45:59.106
and appreciate that that is not something

00:45:59.106 --> 00:46:01.417
parents should have to go through.

00:46:01.417 --> 00:46:02.733
And so when we see that,

00:46:02.733 --> 00:46:05.360
I think it starts to get us to see that

00:46:05.360 --> 00:46:08.380
our social understandings of parenthood

00:46:08.380 --> 00:46:10.503
probably should match
our legal understandings.

00:46:10.503 --> 00:46:14.040
Because if you go to
communities around our state,

00:46:14.040 --> 00:46:16.530
people are treating that
non-biological mother

00:46:16.530 --> 00:46:18.540
in the same sex, couple like a parent,

00:46:18.540 --> 00:46:21.980
the healthcare providers, the
school, the extended family,

00:46:21.980 --> 00:46:23.530
the friends, the neighbors,

00:46:23.530 --> 00:46:25.970
it's just the state that hasn't
been treating her that way.

00:46:25.970 --> 00:46:29.570
And so our law should match
and family law, especially,

00:46:29.570 --> 00:46:30.534
I think it's important.

00:46:30.534 --> 00:46:32.294
we talk about the difference between

00:46:32.294 --> 00:46:35.590
formal family law regimes and
functional family law regimes.

00:46:35.590 --> 00:46:36.780
And I'm on the functional side.

00:46:36.780 --> 00:46:39.024
I think our family law
systems should reflect

00:46:39.024 --> 00:46:41.740
the way people live their lives

00:46:41.740 --> 00:46:43.920
rather than trying to channel people

00:46:43.920 --> 00:46:47.310
into particular forms of
family and relationship

00:46:47.310 --> 00:46:49.892
by punishing people who don't conform.

00:46:49.892 --> 00:46:54.360
And I think less conformity

00:46:54.360 --> 00:46:56.680
around family arrangements
is not a bad thing

00:46:56.680 --> 00:46:59.100
and we should meet people where they are.

00:46:59.100 --> 00:47:02.490
- Where they're, thank
you, Doug, thank you.

00:47:02.490 --> 00:47:06.750
So Doug, I can read one, I
see one question in the chat

00:47:08.140 --> 00:47:10.000
and it's personal.

00:47:10.000 --> 00:47:13.290
So the person said, "This is
a personal question for Doug.

00:47:13.290 --> 00:47:16.700
And the question reads, how
do you organize your time

00:47:16.700 --> 00:47:19.100
and keep energy for your scholarship

00:47:19.100 --> 00:47:22.820
and life outside of
work with this advocacy?

00:47:22.820 --> 00:47:25.480
Any tips on how you do that well?"

00:47:25.480 --> 00:47:27.278
- I'm just exhausted
all the time, I think.

00:47:27.278 --> 00:47:29.330
(both laughing)

00:47:29.330 --> 00:47:31.550
So I have a three year old,

00:47:31.550 --> 00:47:36.070
so that certainly takes a lot of time.

00:47:36.070 --> 00:47:38.333
I mean, I fortunately have the luxury

00:47:38.333 --> 00:47:42.660
of being at a law school
where at least my Dean

00:47:42.660 --> 00:47:47.010
really supported this
work and appreciated it.

00:47:47.010 --> 00:47:52.010
And so if I was a little
less in my scholarly writing,

00:47:52.100 --> 00:47:55.004
I didn't feel like I was shirking,

00:47:55.004 --> 00:47:59.630
but I will also say it was
an intense couple years

00:47:59.630 --> 00:48:04.630
where as any time I had, I was
spending on this legislation.

00:48:05.640 --> 00:48:09.190
And so I fortunately
was able to integrate it

00:48:09.190 --> 00:48:11.740
into my teaching and so when
I say I taught a clinic,

00:48:11.740 --> 00:48:14.200
I also got credit for teaching a clinic.

00:48:14.200 --> 00:48:16.180
And so there were two semesters

00:48:16.180 --> 00:48:19.400
where that was my course
load, teaching the clinic.

00:48:19.400 --> 00:48:22.130
So it really allowed me
to focus on that work.

00:48:22.130 --> 00:48:24.220
The other thing is I think the synergy

00:48:24.220 --> 00:48:27.274
between the legislative advocacy

00:48:27.274 --> 00:48:31.150
and my scholarship is helpful.

00:48:31.150 --> 00:48:33.950
And so I wasn't writing about
things that I was learning

00:48:33.950 --> 00:48:37.160
and getting interested in and
questions were being framed

00:48:37.160 --> 00:48:39.220
by doing that legislative work.

00:48:39.220 --> 00:48:41.350
And so for me, I was fortunate to have,

00:48:41.350 --> 00:48:45.068
and so now it's spawned
this series of projects

00:48:45.068 --> 00:48:49.760
with my co-author because we've
gotten so invested in this.

00:48:49.760 --> 00:48:53.080
But yeah, I was really
happy when it passed

00:48:54.300 --> 00:48:56.550
both because it helped so
many people in the state,

00:48:56.550 --> 00:48:58.380
but also because it meant
I could finally take

00:48:58.380 --> 00:49:00.220
the research leave that I'd been delaying

00:49:00.220 --> 00:49:01.880
because I was working
on this all the time,

00:49:01.880 --> 00:49:03.950
so it's not easy.

00:49:03.950 --> 00:49:07.210
- Fantastic, I see another
question has popped in.

00:49:07.210 --> 00:49:09.840
It says, "You mentioned
one of the testimonies

00:49:09.840 --> 00:49:11.730
from the legislative process.

00:49:11.730 --> 00:49:13.857
Can you share some other stories?"

00:49:15.180 --> 00:49:19.200
- Yeah, so I assume meaning stories

00:49:19.200 --> 00:49:20.945
that people told to lawmakers.

00:49:20.945 --> 00:49:22.530
- That's my assumption.

00:49:22.530 --> 00:49:24.675
- Yeah, so, I mean, actually we had,

00:49:24.675 --> 00:49:27.610
one of the most amazing stories

00:49:27.610 --> 00:49:30.360
was from a Yale Law School
student who was in my clinic

00:49:31.270 --> 00:49:35.130
and she was born through

00:49:35.130 --> 00:49:37.860
one of the first surrogacy
arrangements in the US

00:49:37.860 --> 00:49:40.243
involving a male same-sex couple.

00:49:40.243 --> 00:49:43.500
Her fathers lived in California,

00:49:43.500 --> 00:49:46.030
they then moved to New
Mexico where she was raised.

00:49:46.030 --> 00:49:49.470
And she's been involved with
an advocacy organization

00:49:49.470 --> 00:49:50.600
called Collage,

00:49:50.600 --> 00:49:54.710
which represents two
children of LGBTQ parents.

00:49:54.710 --> 00:49:59.600
And she both got Collage on
board to support our bill

00:49:59.600 --> 00:50:00.740
and now I've been working with them

00:50:00.740 --> 00:50:03.310
and they're involved in the
Massachusetts effort as well,

00:50:03.310 --> 00:50:08.100
but she also testified
and she testified about

00:50:08.100 --> 00:50:11.850
what it was like to grow up
because she's a law student

00:50:11.850 --> 00:50:13.810
and so things changed dramatically

00:50:13.810 --> 00:50:18.810
and not have her non-biologic
father be her legal parent

00:50:20.130 --> 00:50:21.662
to have for a lot of her life

00:50:21.662 --> 00:50:23.410
the woman who was the surrogate,

00:50:23.410 --> 00:50:26.440
be her legal mother on
her birth certificate.

00:50:26.440 --> 00:50:30.510
And to also feel like people
didn't treat her family

00:50:30.510 --> 00:50:32.873
as fully belonging.

00:50:33.880 --> 00:50:37.980
And I had a Republican
legislator on the committee

00:50:37.980 --> 00:50:40.787
call my cell phone after that and said,

00:50:40.787 --> 00:50:43.230
"I don't know if you saw this testimony,

00:50:43.230 --> 00:50:45.800
but it was the most amazing
thing I've ever seen."

00:50:45.800 --> 00:50:50.800
And I could tell, he said by
her testimony that her fathers

00:50:50.880 --> 00:50:52.823
did an incredible job raising her.

00:50:53.810 --> 00:50:56.090
And then when we got to the Senate floor,

00:50:56.090 --> 00:50:59.320
which was our last hurdle
before the Governor's signature,

00:50:59.320 --> 00:51:01.580
our lead Senate sponsor gave a speech

00:51:01.580 --> 00:51:06.140
in which she talked about
the student's testimony

00:51:06.140 --> 00:51:07.300
and about her family.

00:51:07.300 --> 00:51:11.070
And it was so great because

00:51:11.070 --> 00:51:15.040
so much of the Law School
experience at Yale is about

00:51:15.040 --> 00:51:19.370
being analytical and
making arguments in law

00:51:19.370 --> 00:51:21.470
that persuade decision makers.

00:51:21.470 --> 00:51:23.030
And she can do that,

00:51:23.030 --> 00:51:26.231
but she also knew how to make an argument

00:51:26.231 --> 00:51:29.280
that was persuasive for emotional reasons

00:51:29.280 --> 00:51:30.620
and for personal reasons.

00:51:30.620 --> 00:51:33.340
And it also, she started
crying during her testimony

00:51:33.340 --> 00:51:36.097
and she talked to me afterwards and said,

00:51:36.097 --> 00:51:37.560
"I didn't expect that,

00:51:37.560 --> 00:51:39.270
I didn't think I was gonna be so moved

00:51:39.270 --> 00:51:42.980
but telling this group of
lawmakers about my experience

00:51:42.980 --> 00:51:47.010
was just more moving than
I imagined it to be."

00:51:47.010 --> 00:51:48.510
And so it was things like that

00:51:51.190 --> 00:51:53.140
I think that really stuck with me.

00:51:53.140 --> 00:51:56.360
The other thing I'll just
say is there was testimony

00:51:56.360 --> 00:51:58.170
by someone from the Family Law Section

00:51:58.170 --> 00:52:01.240
of the Connecticut Bar in the
first session that we were in

00:52:04.796 --> 00:52:09.030
and what she missed was
that right before her

00:52:09.030 --> 00:52:12.940
was this family I talked
about with the de facto mother

00:52:12.940 --> 00:52:15.000
who had been raising the
kid since he was three

00:52:15.000 --> 00:52:18.180
and now he was 14, and
that had everyone in tears,

00:52:18.180 --> 00:52:20.187
but this woman from the
bar wasn't there for that.

00:52:20.187 --> 00:52:22.267
And so she just waltzed
in when they called her

00:52:22.267 --> 00:52:27.267
and gave this testimony about
how bad defacto parentage was,

00:52:27.670 --> 00:52:30.220
because suddenly anyone
was gonna be a parent,

00:52:30.220 --> 00:52:31.870
you could have 100 parents

00:52:31.870 --> 00:52:33.860
and these people aren't really parents

00:52:33.860 --> 00:52:37.050
and the legislators just tore into her

00:52:37.050 --> 00:52:39.860
and started saying like,
"You mean to tell me this?"

00:52:39.860 --> 00:52:42.610
And then finally the
co-chair of judiciary,

00:52:42.610 --> 00:52:46.560
the Senate side said,
'cause she then kept saying,

00:52:46.560 --> 00:52:47.810
stepparents are gonna be parents.

00:52:47.810 --> 00:52:49.820
And he said, "I'm a stepfather

00:52:49.820 --> 00:52:52.970
and I'm the only thing
other my children know.

00:52:52.970 --> 00:52:54.350
And you have to stop saying

00:52:54.350 --> 00:52:57.040
that all stepparents are not parents."

00:52:57.040 --> 00:53:01.030
So it really was just like
one, know your audience

00:53:01.030 --> 00:53:02.700
and see what comes before you.

00:53:02.700 --> 00:53:06.300
But two, it just showed how
people's actual experiences

00:53:06.300 --> 00:53:09.490
can rebut some of these
arguments that are just about

00:53:09.490 --> 00:53:12.410
assumptions from people
who don't really know

00:53:12.410 --> 00:53:14.651
what people's family lives are like.

00:53:14.651 --> 00:53:19.263
- Yeah and I love in that
story the change in role,

00:53:20.380 --> 00:53:24.480
which the eye perspective that
you were describing, right?

00:53:24.480 --> 00:53:27.030
Yeah, so automatically it became personal.

00:53:27.030 --> 00:53:27.863
- Yes.

00:53:27.863 --> 00:53:29.620
- I do think when things become personal,

00:53:29.620 --> 00:53:34.160
I think that's when we can
make even more progress.

00:53:34.160 --> 00:53:38.880
So Doug, I am trying to
keep my eye on the Q&amp;A

00:53:39.919 --> 00:53:42.340
and we have another question,

00:53:42.340 --> 00:53:43.740
there was a comment where someone said,

00:53:43.740 --> 00:53:45.810
thank you so very for today's talk.

00:53:45.810 --> 00:53:48.140
So I will extend that gratitude to you.

00:53:48.140 --> 00:53:53.010
The next question says, how
intersectional are issues

00:53:53.010 --> 00:53:54.440
about parentage?

00:53:54.440 --> 00:53:56.430
Do you run into questions of racism

00:53:56.430 --> 00:53:58.313
and other forms of discrimination?

00:53:59.340 --> 00:54:02.140
- Yeah, so it's a great question.

00:54:02.140 --> 00:54:07.140
So certainly I think what
fail to realize is that

00:54:09.690 --> 00:54:11.903
if you look at family demographics,

00:54:13.556 --> 00:54:17.640
marital families and non-marital
families look different.

00:54:17.640 --> 00:54:21.700
So we are in a world in
the US of marriage decline,

00:54:21.700 --> 00:54:24.370
rates of marriage used to be high,

00:54:24.370 --> 00:54:25.950
they've gotten lower and lower.

00:54:25.950 --> 00:54:27.430
Rates of marriage used to be similar

00:54:27.430 --> 00:54:29.800
across racial and ethnic groups,

00:54:29.800 --> 00:54:32.160
but they now diverge significantly.

00:54:32.160 --> 00:54:36.850
So African Americans and
Latinos are less likely

00:54:36.850 --> 00:54:41.383
to be married than whites
and Asian Americans.

00:54:42.460 --> 00:54:46.860
It's also stratified by class,
people with higher incomes

00:54:46.860 --> 00:54:50.000
and more education are
more likely to be married.

00:54:50.000 --> 00:54:54.050
And so for us a big part of
this was protecting parents

00:54:54.050 --> 00:54:55.700
and children in non-marital families.

00:54:55.700 --> 00:54:59.093
And so I think that does have
race and class dimensions.

00:55:02.736 --> 00:55:04.730
And I think so that was
important to get people

00:55:04.730 --> 00:55:06.880
to see that look you might think that

00:55:06.880 --> 00:55:08.570
we're just sorting people based on,

00:55:08.570 --> 00:55:09.880
are you a biological parent?

00:55:09.880 --> 00:55:10.940
Are you a marital parent?

00:55:10.940 --> 00:55:13.071
But that actually has a disparate impact

00:55:13.071 --> 00:55:15.403
based on racing class.

00:55:16.530 --> 00:55:18.330
There's also intersections
with immigration.

00:55:18.330 --> 00:55:21.840
And so we did, one of the
sponsors, one of the members

00:55:21.840 --> 00:55:24.490
of our coalition was an
immigration organization

00:55:24.490 --> 00:55:28.930
because I'm sure you remember
early on in our efforts,

00:55:28.930 --> 00:55:30.660
the Trump administration was separating

00:55:30.660 --> 00:55:34.370
children at the border
and the federal government

00:55:34.370 --> 00:55:38.060
decided they were gonna use
DNA tests to determine whether

00:55:38.060 --> 00:55:40.990
people were actually parents of the child.

00:55:40.990 --> 00:55:42.740
Well, to me, that's not determining

00:55:42.740 --> 00:55:44.560
whether someone's actually
a parent of the child.

00:55:44.560 --> 00:55:46.090
And in fact, they weren't even asking

00:55:46.090 --> 00:55:48.260
whether the home jurisdiction
would treat them as a parent.

00:55:48.260 --> 00:55:49.093
It was just sort of,

00:55:49.093 --> 00:55:52.170
we decided this is what a
parent-child relationship is

00:55:52.170 --> 00:55:55.730
and surprise, some of those
people were not genetic parents,

00:55:55.730 --> 00:55:58.170
but they were the primary
caregivers of those children.

00:55:58.170 --> 00:56:01.280
And so people saw the importance

00:56:01.280 --> 00:56:05.120
of more expansive understandings
of family relationships

00:56:05.120 --> 00:56:06.870
in the immigration setting as well.

00:56:08.230 --> 00:56:11.690
- Doug, the last comment
that I see is just that,

00:56:11.690 --> 00:56:13.270
it is another note of gratitude.

00:56:13.270 --> 00:56:15.050
It says, thank you for this session.

00:56:15.050 --> 00:56:16.790
I'm amazed at what I didn't know.

00:56:16.790 --> 00:56:18.000
And you have shed some light

00:56:18.000 --> 00:56:21.020
around unanswered questions that I had.

00:56:21.020 --> 00:56:24.490
And so I'm gonna take to a close

00:56:24.490 --> 00:56:28.110
and first I'm gonna
practice gratitude as well.

00:56:28.110 --> 00:56:29.570
And I wanna say, thank you.

00:56:29.570 --> 00:56:31.970
My thank you are probably
a little bit different.

00:56:31.970 --> 00:56:33.640
Mine is a selfish thank you.

00:56:33.640 --> 00:56:37.080
Because at the beginning you
said you moved to Connecticut.

00:56:37.080 --> 00:56:39.549
So let me say very selfishly,

00:56:39.549 --> 00:56:41.670
I'm so glad that you live in Connecticut

00:56:41.670 --> 00:56:45.570
and that we are working
towards creating a region

00:56:45.570 --> 00:56:47.466
where there is equality.

00:56:47.466 --> 00:56:50.080
But when I start to think about the state

00:56:50.080 --> 00:56:52.320
that I'm living in,
it's really nice to know

00:56:52.320 --> 00:56:54.580
that we're making positive movement

00:56:54.580 --> 00:56:57.130
towards creating a world of inclusion.

00:56:57.130 --> 00:56:59.170
So I'm selfishly saying thank you

00:56:59.170 --> 00:57:03.560
and hope you stay in
Connecticut for quite some time

00:57:03.560 --> 00:57:08.070
to transform our state and us as people.

00:57:08.070 --> 00:57:10.320
You used the word we decided.

00:57:10.320 --> 00:57:13.810
And so I'm gonna switch
that to, I decided,

00:57:13.810 --> 00:57:16.000
I decided that I'm gonna
close out by saying,

00:57:16.000 --> 00:57:20.210
you rock and thank you so much
for all of your time today,

00:57:20.210 --> 00:57:22.840
but I think that thank you
also needs to be extended

00:57:22.840 --> 00:57:27.270
to the team of individuals that
you've constantly itemized,

00:57:27.270 --> 00:57:28.850
as you were answering questions

00:57:28.850 --> 00:57:32.550
that are part of that village
that is helping to make change

00:57:32.550 --> 00:57:34.950
in Connecticut and also in other states

00:57:34.950 --> 00:57:37.850
where change is certainly necessary.

00:57:37.850 --> 00:57:40.540
I just want to close with one question

00:57:40.540 --> 00:57:45.540
and mine is around
hopefulness, as you think about

00:57:45.540 --> 00:57:47.830
the progress that's been
made within our state

00:57:47.830 --> 00:57:52.830
and then you look outside of
Connecticut, US and beyond,

00:57:53.380 --> 00:57:57.770
are you hopeful that
in maybe this lifetime,

00:57:57.770 --> 00:58:01.160
your lifetime or your child's lifetime,

00:58:01.160 --> 00:58:06.160
that we as a society will
become much more inclusive

00:58:06.650 --> 00:58:08.233
and practice more equity?

00:58:09.540 --> 00:58:14.540
- Yeah, so I mean, things are
really depressing right now.

00:58:14.960 --> 00:58:19.960
And I'm a co-author on a case book

00:58:21.100 --> 00:58:23.700
on sexuality and gender
identity and the law

00:58:23.700 --> 00:58:25.340
and we're doing a new addition.

00:58:25.340 --> 00:58:27.550
And my co-author was saying like,

00:58:27.550 --> 00:58:30.960
if you went back to 10 years ago

00:58:30.960 --> 00:58:33.450
and actually the author
who started the book

00:58:33.450 --> 00:58:38.130
in the early '90s said
no idea that these issues

00:58:38.130 --> 00:58:40.730
I wrote about then were
gonna be the main issues now.

00:58:40.730 --> 00:58:43.843
It was anti-gay curriculum laws,

00:58:45.910 --> 00:58:48.660
so that leaves me with a lot of worry.

00:58:48.660 --> 00:58:52.590
But I also think that the
experience here has shown that

00:58:52.590 --> 00:58:55.690
people who are willing
to listen can be moved

00:58:55.690 --> 00:58:59.570
and that people care about their neighbors

00:58:59.570 --> 00:59:02.630
and family members and
more and more people

00:59:02.630 --> 00:59:06.420
are going to be living
in family arrangements

00:59:06.420 --> 00:59:09.320
that don't look like their
parents' family arrangements.

00:59:09.320 --> 00:59:13.370
And I'm an example of having parents

00:59:13.370 --> 00:59:15.870
who come around to understanding

00:59:15.870 --> 00:59:17.850
the importance of respecting people,

00:59:17.850 --> 00:59:21.500
even if they don't conform to
what your life looked like.

00:59:21.500 --> 00:59:24.040
And so I do have some optimism around that

00:59:24.919 --> 00:59:27.480
and seeing the kids in
Florida and Texas push back

00:59:27.480 --> 00:59:28.580
also gives me optimism.

00:59:28.580 --> 00:59:29.613
- Yeah, I agree.

00:59:30.757 --> 00:59:33.080
And our advocates are becoming younger

00:59:33.080 --> 00:59:35.570
and younger and younger,
which I think is fantastic

00:59:35.570 --> 00:59:37.770
and using their voice, not finding it,

00:59:37.770 --> 00:59:39.160
but using their voice.

00:59:39.160 --> 00:59:42.140
So I'm gonna close out again
by saying thank you very much.

00:59:42.140 --> 00:59:47.140
And thank you to all of the
attendees for today's webinar.

00:59:47.180 --> 00:59:49.930
Brief reminder that
Belonging at Yale website

00:59:49.930 --> 00:59:53.530
has a calendar of upcoming
events on topics that

00:59:53.530 --> 00:59:56.200
enhance diversity, support equity,

00:59:56.200 --> 00:59:58.680
and promote a welcoming environment

00:59:58.680 --> 01:00:01.210
and inclusive environment here at Yale.

01:00:01.210 --> 01:00:03.410
Thank you, Doug, for
spending your afternoon

01:00:03.410 --> 01:00:05.610
and almost a little bit
of your evening with us

01:00:05.610 --> 01:00:07.240
and everyone else, thank you so much

01:00:07.240 --> 01:00:10.600
and have a great evening and
a great weekend, take care.

01:00:10.600 --> 01:00:11.463
- Thank you.

